# Playwright and Lighthouse

## Environment

Use Node 24. The Nix shell gives Node 24 and the Chromium of nixpkgs:

    nix develop .#frontend

The browsers that Playwright downloads do not run on NixOS. The shell sets
`BROWSER_PATH` and `CHROME_PATH` to Chromium, and it sets
`PLAYWRIGHT_SKIP_BROWSER_DOWNLOAD`. Playwright reads `BROWSER_PATH`.
Lighthouse reads `CHROME_PATH`. Outside the shell, set `BROWSER_PATH` yourself:

    BROWSER_PATH=$(which chromium) npm run e2e:flows

The tests use the built app in `dist/pilzkarte/browser`. Build it first:

    npm run build:e2e

## Port

`e2e/serve.mjs` serves the build on port 4400. Outside CI, Playwright uses a
server that already listens on that port. Thus two runs at the same time (for
example in two worktrees) can test the wrong build. Give each parallel run a
unique port:

    E2E_PORT=4410 npm run e2e:flows

## Projects

- `phone` and `wide` compare the boards with the baselines in `boards/baseline/`.
- `blocks` compares the cards of the building blocks on `/dev/blocks`. This route exists only in the build of `npm run build:e2e`.
- `desktop` tests only `flows/desktop/`.
- `flows` tests the flows of `artefakte/nfr.md`.

## Board baselines

The source of each board baseline and each component baseline is
`artefakte/mockups/design/uebergabe/`. Run `npm run boards:sync` to copy the
current images into `boards/baseline/`. The command also writes
`boards/baseline/SOURCE.json`, with the SHA-256 of each copied file.

Run `npm run boards:check` before a commit. It compares each baseline with
`SOURCE.json`. It fails if a file is different or not in the list. This check
makes sure that no baseline comes from the app itself.

Do not edit a file under `boards/baseline/` by hand.

## Service worker

A service worker catches the requests before a mock can answer them. Thus
`playwright.config.ts` blocks it for all tests. The installation test allows
it again with `test.use({ serviceWorkers: 'allow' })`.

## Lighthouse: category PWA

Lighthouse 11 is pinned, because Lighthouse 12 does not have the category PWA.

The category PWA stops the job: `lighthouserc.json` fails below 0.9, and the
job `fe-lighthouse` has no `continue-on-error`. The measured score is 1.0
(installable manifest, splash screen, theme colour, viewport, maskable icon).

## Lighthouse: category performance

The category performance gives a warning below 0.9 until U12. The measured
score is 0.53 in CI and 0.89 on a local computer. The same build without
this package also gives 0.89 locally. The difference comes from the CI
computer: the Total Blocking Time is 1,570 ms there and 140 ms locally. U12
changes the rule to `error`.

## Map boards

Each of the thirteen boards of the page `map` has a test in
`boards/map.spec.ts`. The test blocks the base map. It puts the map image
from `boards/fixtures` on the map area, so the image is the same at each run.
The images are in Git in `boards/fixtures`.

There is one map image for each map height: `map-<species>-<height>.png`, at
the top left, without scale. The height is the free strip above the sheet:
660 when the sheet is closed, 470 when it is half open, 300 when it is fully
open, 900 on a desktop.

The boards are still in `pending.json`. The difference is 2 to 9 %, and the
tolerance is 0.5 %. The pull request tells what is missing.

## Species boards

Each of the fourteen boards of the page `species` has a test in
`boards/species.spec.ts`. Each test sets its own catalogue:

- `fixtures/species.ts` builds a species by the contract.
- `fixtures/species-boards.ts` holds the data of each board.
- `fixtures/species-catalogue.ts` builds the catalogue with 306 species. Its counts agree with the boards of the filter.
- `fixtures/filter-state.ts` puts the filter selection into the storage before the page starts.

The rows have no images. The bundle gives `leadPhotoId`, and
`app-species-row` shows the placeholder from the cap colours of the species.
